.TH NODEX 1 "2026-10-08" "nodex 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nodex \- local CLI for inspecting and operating self-hosted infrastructure
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nodex
[GLOBAL-FLAGS]
COMMAND
[COMMAND-ARGS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Nodex
is a local, single-process command-line program for Proxmox VE and Proxmox
Backup Server. Optional workflows provide explicitly enrolled Linux fleet
maintenance through an allowlisted Ansible adapter and one-shot checks against
configured targets.
.PP
Nodex has no daemon or agent installed on managed hosts, no telemetry, and no
continuous monitoring service. The opt-in --agent flag selects a structured
local CLI interface; it is not a sandbox or proof of human authorization.
.PP
Use nodex help COMMAND, nodex COMMAND --help, and docs/cli-reference.md for
complete command syntax. Use nodex operation list --output json and nodex
operation describe PATH --output json for machine-readable operation and safety
discovery.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.B --profile NAME
Select a configured provider profile. Agent-mode remote operations require an
explicit profile.
.TP
.B --output FORMAT
Output format: table, json, or yaml. Defaults to table on a terminal and JSON
when redirected.
.TP
.B --timeout DURATION
Provider request timeout (default: 30s).
.TP
.B --limit N
Limit output items (0 means no limit).
.TP
.B --all
Aggregate the supported read-only commands status, node list, vm list and
container list across configured profiles.
.TP
.B --non-interactive
Disable prompts. Operations fail closed when required confirmations are
missing.
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress non-essential logger output.
.TP
.B --verbose
Enable informational diagnostics on stderr.
.TP
.B --debug
Enable debug diagnostics on stderr; sensitive values are redacted.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable color output.
.TP
.B --agent
Use opt-in structured JSON agent execution for operations whose contracts
declare support.
.TP
.B --request-id ID
Agent request identifier. Mutations use it for local deduplication; IDs are not
credentials.
.SH MUTATION FLAGS
.TP
.B --yes
Confirm operations in the reversible tier.
.TP
.B --force
Additional acknowledgement for disruptive operations; requires --yes.
.TP
.B --confirm-target TEXT
Supply the exact operation-specific target confirmation for destructive
operations in non-interactive use.
.TP
.B --expert
Enable security-administration commands. It does not replace any other
operation-specific confirmation.
.TP
.B --wait
Wait for a provider task where supported. Task completion does not necessarily
prove the requested resource state.
.TP
.B --password-stdin
Read a password from stdin for commands that request one. Passwords are not
provider authentication for PVE or PBS.
.SH SAFETY TIERS
.TP
.B Tier 0 (Observation)
Read-only operation; no mutation confirmation.
.TP
.B Tier 1 (Reversible)
Requires --yes or interactive confirmation.
.TP
.B Tier 2 (Disruptive)
Requires --yes --force or the operation's interactive confirmation.
.TP
.B Tier 3 (Destructive)
Requires the operation's target confirmation, plus any other listed flags.
.TP
.B Tier 4 (Security administration)
Requires --expert, plus any other listed flags.
.PP
The live operation registry provides the exact confirmation target and safety
requirements for each operation. In non-interactive mode, missing requirements
are rejected. Confirmation flags acknowledge intent but do not prove that a
human approved the action.
.SH COMMAND GROUPS
.TP
.B profile, provider, setup, init
Configure and diagnose local provider profiles and credentials. See
docs/configuration.md.
.TP
.B node, vm, container, storage, task, status, cluster, event, log
Inspect and operate Proxmox VE resources, tasks and host/cluster state.
.TP
.B backup, pbs, environment
Manage PVE backups, inspect/run guarded PBS maintenance, and evaluate paired
PVE/PBS environments and guest backup coverage.
.TP
.B firewall, ha, sdn, pools, network, access, ceph, replication
Inspect and, where implemented, manage Proxmox policy, topology, identity and
storage-related resources. Some operations require --expert or stronger
confirmation.
.TP
.B maintenance
Inspect enrolled Linux hosts, create and apply immutable update plans, and
verify or reconcile durable receipts. Requires Ansible. Maintenance plans
currently use a never reboot policy.
.TP
.B monitor
Run explicitly configured checks once. Nodex does not schedule checks, retain
history or send alerts.
.TP
.B operation, agent
Discover command contracts and opt in to structured agent execution with local
mutation receipts. See docs/agent-interface.md and NODEX_AGENT.md.
.TP
.B certification
Run or recover opt-in disposable-environment certification. Never point
disposable mutation certification at production infrastructure.
.TP
.B completion, version, help
Generate shell completion, inspect version information and display command
help.
.SH OUTPUT
.PP
Use JSON or YAML for scripts; table output is intended for people and its
layout may change. Structured output contains no human-readable status text.
Mutation commands report submission, task outcome and postcondition
verification as separate facts. An accepted asynchronous request is not
completed work. See docs/cli-reference.md for result envelopes and exit codes.
.SH CONFIGURATION AND CREDENTIALS
.PP
Configuration is a YAML file under the platform's standard Nodex configuration
directory. Profiles name the provider, HTTPS endpoint, optional CA/SSH trust
settings and a credential reference. Credentials can come from a file, OS
keyring, environment variables or stdin. PVE and PBS tokens are separate.
Never place live secrets in command arguments, configuration examples or
shell history. See docs/configuration.md and SECURITY.md.
.SH SECURITY
.PP
Provider endpoints require HTTPS with certificate and hostname verification;
there is no insecure TLS option. API mutations are protected by operation
metadata and confirmation gates. Ansible is constrained to allowlisted
operations with SSH host-key checking enabled. Agent receipts are local
recovery/deduplication data, not authorization evidence. See
docs/threat-model.md.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/nodex/config.yaml
Linux default configuration path; XDG_CONFIG_HOME may override it.
.TP
.B ~/.nodex/credentials/
Default file credential directory.
.PP
On macOS configuration is under ~/Library/Application Support/Nodex/. On
Windows it is under AppData/Nodex/. Exact platform paths and state directories
are documented in docs/configuration.md.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NODEX_PROFILE_TOKEN_ID
API token ID for a profile (profile name uppercased; hyphens become
underscores).
.TP
.B NODEX_PROFILE_TOKEN_SECRET
API token secret for a profile.
.TP
.B NODEX_PROFILE_TOKEN
Combined token value when supported by the credential resolver.
.TP
.B NODEX_PROFILE_USERNAME
Username credential field.
.TP
.B NODEX_PROFILE_PASSWORD
Password credential field for commands that request one; not PVE/PBS API
authentication.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
Exit status 0 indicates success. Nonzero codes classify usage/configuration,
credential, authentication/authorization, network/TLS, provider, task,
validation, timeout, cancellation, ambiguous outcome, rate limit, conflict and
output failures. SIGINT exits 130 and SIGTERM exits 143. See the complete
stable code table in docs/cli-reference.md.
.SH DOCUMENTATION
.PP
README.md is the project overview. Further docs live under docs/: operator-guide.md,
cli-reference.md, configuration.md, architecture.md, agent-interface.md,
maintenance.md, compatibility.md, threat-model.md, roadmap.md, and releasing.md.
